(window.DETAIL_C = window.DETAIL_C || {})["chem-12-03"] = {"chem-12-03-m01":{"idea":"A strong electrolyte ionises almost completely in water. Sort the four substances by this.","steps":["NaCl is an ionic salt. In water it splits almost fully into Na⁺ and Cl⁻.","So it is a strong electrolyte.","Acetic acid and ammonium hydroxide ionise only a little, so they are weak.","Glucose does not form ions at all."],"others":"Acetic acid and ammonium hydroxide are weak electrolytes. Glucose is a non-electrolyte, so it cannot ionise almost completely, whatever the choice says.","answer":"sodium chloride, which ionises almost completely"},"chem-12-03-m02":{"idea":"Degree of dissociation tells how much of a dissolved substance has split into ions.","steps":["If c is the starting concentration and α the degree of dissociation, the ions have concentration αc.","α is the fraction of molecules that have ionised.","For a weak electrolyte α is much less than 1."],"others":"The number of ions per molecule is a different thing. A ratio of two ion concentrations is not α. The concentration of ions in mol/L is αc, not α.","answer":"the fraction of the dissolved molecules that have ionised"},"chem-12-03-m03":{"idea":"Conjugate base = acid minus one proton.","steps":["Write the acid losing one H⁺: H₂SO₄ → H⁺ + HSO₄⁻.","What remains is HSO₄⁻.","So HSO₄⁻ is the conjugate base of H₂SO₄."],"others":"SO₄²⁻ is two protons lost, so it is the conjugate base of HSO₄⁻. H₃SO₄⁺ is H₂SO₄ plus a proton, so it is the conjugate acid. OH⁻ is not related to H₂SO₄.","trap":"Remove one H⁺ only, to get the conjugate base.","answer":"HSO₄⁻"},"chem-12-03-m04":{"idea":"Conjugate acid = base plus one proton.","steps":["Ammonia accepts a proton.","NH₃ + H⁺ → NH₄⁺.","So NH₄⁺ is the conjugate acid."],"others":"NH₂⁻ is NH₃ losing a proton, so it is the conjugate base. NH₄OH is the hydrated form, not the result of adding a proton. N₂H₄ is hydrazine.","answer":"NH₄⁺"},"chem-12-03-m05":{"idea":"Amphoteric means able to act as both an acid and a base.","steps":["With a base such as NH₃, water gives H⁺ and acts as an acid.","With an acid such as HCl, water takes H⁺ and acts as a base.","So it behaves as both."],"others":"Acting only as a base or only as an acid ignores the other reaction. Water is not inert to acids and bases.","answer":"can act as an acid towards a base, and as a base towards an acid"},"chem-12-03-m06":{"idea":"Lewis acid = electron pair acceptor. Brønsted–Lowry acid = proton donor.","steps":["Boron in BF₃ has six electrons in its outer shell.","It can accept an electron pair, so it is a Lewis acid.","BF₃ has no hydrogen atom, so it cannot give a proton.","So it is not a Brønsted–Lowry acid."],"others":"A Brønsted acid must give a proton. BF₃ has none, so choices that say it has a proton to give are wrong. Giving a pair of electrons is the behaviour of a Lewis base.","answer":"can accept a pair of electrons, but it has no proton to give"},"chem-12-03-m07":{"idea":"Ostwald's dilution law links the degree of dissociation to concentration.","steps":["K = α²c / (1 − α).","For a weak electrolyte α is small, so 1 − α ≈ 1 and K = α²c.","α = √[K/c].","When c falls (dilution), α rises."],"others":"α = K/c has no square root. α = √[Kc] puts c in the wrong place. √[K/c] is right, but α does not decrease on dilution.","answer":"α = √(K/c), so it increases when the solution is diluted"},"chem-12-03-m08":{"idea":"Two things change on dilution: α and the ion concentration. They move in opposite ways.","steps":["α = √[K/c] rises as c falls.","[H⁺] = αc = √[Kc].","Since c is smaller, √[Kc] is smaller.","So α increases but [H⁺] decreases."],"others":"Both rising is wrong, since [H⁺] falls. α does not decrease on dilution. Unchanged values would mean dilution has no effect, which is not true.","trap":"More dilute does not mean more H⁺.","answer":"the degree of dissociation increases, but the H⁺ concentration decreases"},"chem-12-03-m09":{"idea":"K_w is the product of the two ion concentrations, so it has squared units.","steps":["K_w = [H₃O⁺][OH⁻].","In pure water at 298 K, each ion is 10⁻⁷ mol/dm³.","K_w = 10⁻⁷ × 10⁻⁷ = 10⁻¹⁴.","Units: (mol/dm³)² = mol² dm⁻⁶."],"others":"1.0 × 10¹⁴ has the wrong sign of the power. Units of mol dm⁻³ are for a single concentration, not a product. 10⁻⁷ is each ion's concentration, not K_w.","answer":"1.0 × 10⁻¹⁴ mol² dm⁻⁶"},"chem-12-03-m10":{"idea":"HCl is a strong acid, so it ionises fully.","steps":["[H⁺] = 1.0 × 10⁻³ M.","pH = −log[H⁺] = −log(10⁻³).","So the pH of this solution is 3."],"others":"pH 1 would need 0.1 M. 0.001 is the concentration, not the pH. 11 is the pH of a base of the same strength.","trap":"pH = the power of 10 when [H⁺] is a power of 10.","answer":"3"},"chem-12-03-m11":{"idea":"NaOH is a strong base. Find pOH first.","steps":["[OH⁻] = 0.010 = 10⁻² M.","pOH = −log(10⁻²) = 2.","pH = 14 − pOH = 14 − 2 = 12."],"others":"pH 2 would be for an acid of the same strength. pH 10 would need [OH⁻] = 10⁻⁴. pH 13 would need 0.1 M NaOH.","answer":"12"},"chem-12-03-m12":{"idea":"pH is a log scale, so each pH unit is a factor of 10.","steps":["[H₃O⁺] at pH 3 = 10⁻³ M. At pH 5 it is 10⁻⁵ M.","Ratio = 10⁻³ / 10⁻⁵ = 10².","So it is 100 times as large."],"others":"2 times counts the pH difference as simple subtraction. 10 times is the ratio for one unit. 1000 times is the ratio for three units.","answer":"100 times as large"},"chem-12-03-m13":{"idea":"Neutral means [H⁺] = [OH⁻], not pH 7.","steps":["K_w increases with temperature.","In pure water [H⁺] = [OH⁻] = √K_w, which is larger at 373 K.","So the pH is below 7, here about 6.","But the two concentrations are equal, so water is neutral."],"others":"pH below 7 does not mean acidic when the temperature is not 298 K. [H⁺] greater than [OH⁻] is acidic, not neutral. Water cannot be basic with pH below 7 here.","answer":"neutral, because [H⁺] is equal to [OH⁻]"},"chem-12-03-m14":{"idea":"Find which ion of the salt reacts with water.","steps":["CH₃COONa gives Na⁺ and CH₃COO⁻.","Acetate is the conjugate base of a weak acid, so it takes H⁺ from water: CH₃COO⁻ + H₂O ⇌ CH₃COOH + OH⁻.","OH⁻ is left in excess, so the solution is basic.","Na⁺ comes from a strong base and does not react."],"others":"The salt is fully ionised, not partly. Na⁺ does not take OH⁻. Acetate does not give H⁺ to water.","answer":"the acetate ion takes H⁺ from water, and this leaves OH⁻ in excess"},"chem-12-03-m15":{"idea":"Find which ion of NH₄Cl reacts with water.","steps":["NH₄Cl gives NH₄⁺ and Cl⁻.","NH₄⁺ is the conjugate acid of a weak base, so it gives H⁺: NH₄⁺ + H₂O ⇌ NH₃ + H₃O⁺.","Cl⁻ comes from a strong acid and does not react.","So H₃O⁺ is in excess and the solution is acidic."],"others":"Cl⁻ does not give H⁺. NH₄⁺ gives H⁺, so the solution is not basic. A salt of a strong acid with a weak base is acidic, not neutral.","trap":"Salt of strong acid + weak base is acidic.","answer":"acidic, as NH₄⁺ gives H⁺ to water"},"chem-12-03-m16":{"idea":"Both ions of ammonium acetate hydrolyse, and their effects can cancel.","steps":["CH₃COO⁻ makes the solution basic.","NH₄⁺ makes the solution acidic.","The acid strength of acetic acid and the base strength of ammonia are nearly equal.","So the two effects cancel and pH ≈ 7."],"others":"The salt does hydrolyse, so no hydrolysis is wrong. It is a salt of a weak acid and a weak base, not strong ones. If K_a were much greater than K_b, the solution would be acidic.","answer":"K_a of acetic acid and K_b of ammonia are nearly equal"},"chem-12-03-m17":{"idea":"A buffer is defined by what it does when acid or base is added.","steps":["A buffer is a weak acid with its salt, or a weak base with its salt.","If a little acid or base is added, the pH changes very little.","So it resists a change in pH."],"others":"A buffer does not change pH in proportion to additions. Its pH is not always 7. It contains ions, as it has a salt.","answer":"resists a change in pH when a little acid or base is added"},"chem-12-03-m18":{"idea":"Use the Henderson–Hasselbalch equation.","steps":["pH = pK_a + log([salt]/[acid]).","The concentrations are equal, so the ratio is 1.","log 1 = 0.","So pH = pK_a."],"others":"14 − pK_a belongs to a different relation. Twice pK_a is not in the equation. 7 is only true for special cases, and an acidic buffer is below 7.","answer":"equal to the pKa of the acid"},"chem-12-03-m19":{"idea":"Dilution changes both buffer components by the same factor.","steps":["pH = pK_a + log([salt]/[acid]).","Doubling the volume halves both concentrations.","The ratio [salt]/[acid] does not change.","So the pH stays almost the same, though the buffer capacity falls."],"others":"The pH does not rise or fall by 0.3, since that would need the ratio to change. Water does not make a buffer neutral.","answer":"stays almost the same, as the ratio [salt]/[acid] is unchanged"},"chem-12-03-m20":{"idea":"Write the dissolution equation, then put in the ion concentrations.","steps":["PbI₂ ⇌ Pb²⁺ + 2I⁻.","[Pb²⁺] = S and [I⁻] = 2S.","K_sp = [Pb²⁺][I⁻]² = S × (2S)².","K_sp = 4S³."],"others":"S² is for a salt that gives one cation and one anion, like AgCl. 2S³ forgets to square the 2. 27S⁴ is for salts like Al(OH)₃.","trap":"Square the concentration of the ion with coefficient 2, including the 2.","answer":"K_sp = 4S³"},"chem-12-03-m21":{"idea":"Same method: write the equation and the ion concentrations.","steps":["Al(OH)₃ ⇌ Al³⁺ + 3OH⁻.","[Al³⁺] = S and [OH⁻] = 3S.","K_sp = [Al³⁺][OH⁻]³ = S × (3S)³.","K_sp = 27S⁴."],"others":"9S³ and 4S³ have the wrong power of S. 3S⁴ forgets to cube the 3. The 4S³ form is for salts such as PbI₂.","answer":"K_sp = 27S⁴"},"chem-12-03-m22":{"idea":"Compare the ionic product with K_sp.","steps":["Ionic product = [cation][anion] with their powers, using the actual concentrations.","If it is greater than K_sp, the solution holds too many ions.","The extra ions come out as solid.","So a precipitate forms when the ionic product > K_sp."],"others":"Less than K_sp means unsaturated, so nothing precipitates. The square root of K_sp or half of K_sp are not conditions for precipitation.","answer":"greater than K_sp"},"chem-12-03-m23":{"idea":"Common ion effect: an ion already present reduces solubility.","steps":["AgCl(s) ⇌ Ag⁺ + Cl⁻.","NaCl adds more Cl⁻.","The equilibrium shifts left, so less AgCl dissolves.","K_sp is the same, but solubility is lower."],"others":"Common ion effect lowers solubility, so more is wrong. K_sp is constant, but that does not mean solubility is the same. NaCl being a strong electrolyte is the source of the common ion, not a reason for more solubility.","answer":"less, because of the common ion effect of Cl⁻"},"chem-12-03-m24":{"idea":"K_sp depends only on temperature, but solubility depends on ion concentrations.","steps":["Temperature is fixed, so K_sp is fixed.","Adding a common ion raises one ion concentration.","To keep the product equal to K_sp, the other ion must fall.","So more salt precipitates and solubility decreases."],"others":"K_sp does not decrease or increase at constant temperature. Solubility does not increase with a common ion.","answer":"remains the same, but the solubility decreases"},"chem-12-03-m25":{"idea":"NH₄Cl gives a common ion, NH₄⁺, to the weak base NH₄OH.","steps":["NH₄OH ⇌ NH₄⁺ + OH⁻.","NH₄Cl adds NH₄⁺.","The equilibrium shifts left.","The ionisation of the base is suppressed, so [OH⁻] decreases."],"others":"NH₄Cl is not inert here, because it supplies a common ion. OH⁻ does not increase due to NH₄⁺. The salt does not give OH⁻ when it ionises.","answer":"decreases, due to the common ion effect of NH₄⁺"},"chem-12-03-n01":{"idea":"NaOH is a strong base, so it ionises fully.","steps":["[OH⁻] = 1.0 × 10⁻³ M.","pOH = −log(10⁻³) = 3.","pH = 14 − pOH = 14 − 3.","pH = 11.00."],"trap":"A base must give pH above 7.","answer":"11.00"},"chem-12-03-n02":{"idea":"For a weak acid, α is small, so use α = √[K_a/c].","steps":["α = √(1.0 × 10⁻⁵ / 0.10) = √(10⁻⁴) = 0.01.","[H⁺] = αc = 0.01 × 0.10 = 1.0 × 10⁻³ M.","pH = −log(10⁻³) = 3.00."],"trap":"Weak acid at 0.1 M should give pH above 1.","answer":"3.00"},"chem-12-03-n03":{"idea":"Use Ostwald's law with small α: α = √[K_a/c].","steps":["K_a = 1.0 × 10⁻⁶ and c = 0.010 M.","α = √(1.0 × 10⁻⁶ / 0.010) = √(10⁻⁴).","α = 0.010."],"trap":"α must be less than 1 for a weak acid.","answer":"0.010"},"chem-12-03-n04":{"idea":"Use the Henderson–Hasselbalch equation for an acidic buffer.","steps":["pH = pK_a + log([salt]/[acid]).","Ratio = 0.10 / 0.20 = 0.5, and log 0.5 = −0.301.","pH = 4.74 − 0.301.","pH = 4.44."],"trap":"More acid than salt makes pH lower than pK_a.","answer":"4.44"},"chem-12-03-n05":{"idea":"Strong base reacts with the weak acid in the buffer. Find the new amounts first.","steps":["CH₃COOH + OH⁻ → CH₃COO⁻ + H₂O.","Acid left = 0.10 − 0.010 = 0.09 mol. Salt = 0.10 + 0.010 = 0.11 mol.","pH = 4.74 + log(0.11 / 0.09) = 4.74 + 0.087.","pH = 4.83."],"trap":"The pH rose by only 0.09 for the added NaOH, which shows buffer action.","answer":"4.83"},"chem-12-03-n06":{"idea":"PbI₂ gives 1 Pb²⁺ and 2 I⁻, so K_sp = 4S³.","steps":["S³ = K_sp / 4 = 1.4 × 10⁻⁸ / 4 = 3.5 × 10⁻⁹.","S = (3.5 × 10⁻⁹)^(1/3).","S = 1.52 × 10⁻³ mol/L.","In units of 10⁻³ mol/L, S = 1.52."],"trap":"Check: 4 × (1.52 × 10⁻³)³ ≈ 1.4 × 10⁻⁸.","answer":"1.52 × 10⁻³ mol/L"},"chem-12-03-n07":{"idea":"Mixing doubles the volume, so each concentration is halved. Then find the ionic product.","steps":["[Pb²⁺] = 2.0 × 10⁻³ / 2 = 1.0 × 10⁻³ M.","[I⁻] = 2.0 × 10⁻³ / 2 = 1.0 × 10⁻³ M.","Ionic product = [Pb²⁺][I⁻]² = (10⁻³)(10⁻³)² = 1.0 × 10⁻⁹.","It is less than K_sp = 1.4 × 10⁻⁸, so no precipitate forms."],"trap":"Do not forget to square [I⁻].","answer":"1.0 × 10⁻⁹"},"chem-12-03-n08":{"idea":"The common ion Cl⁻ from NaCl is much larger than the Cl⁻ from AgCl.","steps":["[Cl⁻] ≈ 0.10 M, since S is tiny.","K_sp = S × 0.10, so S = K_sp / 0.10.","S = 1.8 × 10⁻¹⁰ / 0.10 = 1.8 × 10⁻⁹ mol/L.","In pure water S is 1.3 × 10⁻⁵, so common ion lowers it a lot."],"trap":"The answer is much smaller than the pure-water solubility.","answer":"1.8 × 10⁻⁹ mol/L"},"chem-12-03-n09":{"idea":"NH₄⁺ hydrolyses and gives H⁺. Find K_h first.","steps":["K_h = K_w / K_b = 1.0 × 10⁻¹⁴ / 1.8 × 10⁻⁵ = 5.56 × 10⁻¹⁰.","[H⁺] = √[K_h c] = √(5.56 × 10⁻¹⁰ × 0.10) = 7.45 × 10⁻⁶ M.","pH = −log(7.45 × 10⁻⁶).","pH = 5.13."],"trap":"A salt of a weak base and strong acid gives pH below 7.","answer":"5.13"},"chem-12-03-n10":{"idea":"Strong acid and strong base: find which is in excess, then use its concentration.","steps":["Moles H⁺ = 0.050 L × 0.10 = 5.0 × 10⁻³ mol.","Moles OH⁻ = 0.050 L × 0.050 = 2.5 × 10⁻³ mol.","Excess H⁺ = 2.5 × 10⁻³ mol in 0.100 L, so [H⁺] = 0.025 M.","pH = −log(0.025) = 1.60."],"trap":"The total volume is 100 mL, not 50 mL.","answer":"1.60"},"chem-12-03-n11":{"idea":"Use pH to get [H⁺], then K_a = [H⁺]² / (c − [H⁺]).","steps":["[H⁺] = 10⁻³ M, so acid left = 0.10 − 0.001 = 0.099 M.","K_a = (10⁻³)² / 0.099.","K_a = 1.01 × 10⁻⁵.","In units of 10⁻⁵ this is 1.01."],"trap":"Check with α = 0.01: K_a = α²c/(1 − α) = 1.01 × 10⁻⁵.","answer":"1.01 × 10⁻⁵"},"chem-12-03-n12":{"idea":"Mix by adding moles of H⁺, not by averaging pH.","steps":["Moles H⁺ = 10⁻² × 0.1 + 10⁻⁴ × 0.1 = 1.01 × 10⁻³ mol.","Total volume = 0.2 L.","[H⁺] = 1.01 × 10⁻³ / 0.2 = 5.05 × 10⁻³ M.","pH = −log(5.05 × 10⁻³) = 2.30."],"trap":"The average of 2 and 4 is 3.00, but that is wrong because pH is logarithmic.","answer":"2.30"}};
